# SD-LEO-INFRA-CHANGELOG-CONTENTION-PARALLEL-001 — parallel fleet worker sessions hand-edit the
# single CHANGELOG.md at merge time, colliding whenever two sessions concurrently append entries
# under the same date/category section (105 commits touched it in 30d, ~31% of repo churn).
# git's built-in `union` merge driver unions non-conflicting textual changes instead of raising a
# conflict; measured to resolve the exact reported concurrent-append shape cleanly (both entries
# preserved, correctly nested, zero conflict markers).
#
# REAL PRECONDITION (measured, not assumed — an earlier draft of this comment wrongly said "must
# be in the merge-base"; that is FALSE): git reads .gitattributes from the CHECKED-OUT ("ours")
# side's working tree/history AT MERGE TIME, not from the merge-base commit. A branch forked
# before this line lands on main still gets ONE conflict on its first `git merge origin/main`
# (its own checked-out history lacks this line at that moment) — clean thereafter, once that sync
# brings the line into its own tip. This matches the existing, already-required pre-ship
# main-sync workflow; no new process burden.
#
# ACCEPTED RESIDUAL RISK: union merge trades a loud conflict for a silent-duplication risk if two
# sessions ever REWORD the SAME existing entry (rather than each appending a distinct new one) —
# both copies would be kept instead of forcing a human decision. Low frequency (changelog entries
# are rarely edited post-hoc); revisit with a per-SD fragment+assembler design only if this is
# ever actually observed in practice. Also out of scope: GitHub's server-side PR-merge button does
# not apply .gitattributes merge drivers to its own conflict detection — this fix covers local
# `git merge`/`git rebase` (the actual source of the reported pain: shipping prep, main-sync
# before /ship), not the server-side path.
#
# Anchored to root (`/CHANGELOG.md`, not a bare `CHANGELOG.md`) — an unanchored pattern matches
# that basename at any depth (SECURITY review, EXEC-TO-PLAN). Today a no-op (this is the only
# tracked CHANGELOG.md), but anchoring makes the intent explicit and avoids a future vendored or
# sub-package CHANGELOG.md silently inheriting union merging.
/CHANGELOG.md merge=union

# SD-LEO-INFRA-UNIFY-VENTURE-STAGE-001-D
# Pin the generated stage-config.js to LF so its on-disk form matches the
# generator output (scripts/generate-stage-config.cjs emits LF) on every
# platform. Without this, core.autocrlf=true rewrites it to CRLF on Windows
# checkouts, making `npm run venture-stages:check` fail spuriously (this was
# the pre-existing ~699-line "drift" Child D fixed).
lib/proving-companion/stage-config.js eol=lf

# SD-FDBK-INFRA-CLAUDE-LEAD-EXCEEDS-001 — these files sit within ~4% of the Read tool 25k
# single-call cap. core.autocrlf=true rewrites LF to CRLF on Windows checkouts, which adds
# exactly one byte per line: +1372 on CLAUDE_LEAD.md alone, ~568 tokens, 58% of its margin —
# from a git operation that changes no content. Observed for real during this SD after a
# stash round-trip. Pin LF so a checkout cannot silently push a fixed file back over cap.
CLAUDE_LEAD.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE_LEAD_MANUAL.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE_PLAN.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE_PLAN_MANUAL.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE_CORE.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE_EXEC.md text eol=lf
CLAUDE.md text eol=lf

# SD-FDBK-ENH-LEARNING-LOOP-DESTROYS-001 — ESLint auto-fix rewrites staged JS, and with
# core.autocrlf=true that rewrite converts LF to CRLF. It turned a 60-line change into a
# 1070-line diff (546 CRLF pairs), which is unreviewable and hides the real edit. Same class
# as the CLAUDE_*.md pins above, on the source tree this time.
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf

# SD-FDBK-ENH-LEARNING-LOOP-DESTROYS-001 FR-5 — .sql was omitted when the pins above were added,
# and migrations are the one file type where the line endings are load-bearing: the DDL approval
# gate binds an `-- @approved-by:` marker to a CONTENT HASH. Measured on this very migration:
# blob at HEAD 7c3e6fab, blob at the chairman-approved commit 0f9d0e3c, and the working file on
# disk c731a7ad — a third value matching no recorded hash. So an approver or auditor who runs
# sha256sum on the checked-out file concludes the approved content drifted when nothing changed.
# Pin LF so the worktree and the object store agree.
#
# This pin only helps repos that have it. Tooling that hashes a migration for approval should
# still read the blob (git show <rev>:<path>) rather than the worktree.
*.sql text eol=lf

# SD-LEO-INFRA-REPO-WIDE-GITATTRIBUTES-001 — Phase 1 extension of eol=lf pinning to text-file
# types that were, prior to this SD, entirely unmanaged and vulnerable to the same
# core.autocrlf=true silent-CRLF-rewrite class the pins above already fix for .js/.md/.sql.
# Each of these 15 extensions was independently re-measured at EXEC time (not just LEAD-phase)
# via `git ls-files --eol` and confirmed 0 crlf / 0 mixed repo-wide — see
# docs/audits/SD-LEO-INFRA-REPO-WIDE-GITATTRIBUTES-001-census.md for the full per-extension
# receipt. Extensions carrying existing crlf/mixed debt (.md, .ts, .json, .sh, .html,
# .gitignore, .toml) are deliberately EXCLUDED — adding eol=lf over an already-CRLF/mixed
# blob makes that file permanently, irreducibly dirty (git status shows modified with zero
# content change; restore/checkout -- cannot clean it). Do NOT add those extensions here
# without first driving their crlf/mixed count to 0.
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.mmd text eol=lf
*.ps1 text eol=lf
*.partial text eol=lf
*.csv text eol=lf
*.patch text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
*.intoto text eol=lf
*.gitkeep text eol=lf
*.map text eol=lf
*.tsx text eol=lf
*.example text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.template text eol=lf

# SD-LEO-INFRA-REPO-WIDE-GITATTRIBUTES-001 — mark genuinely binary extensions `binary`
# (= -text -diff) as a defensive pin (these already carry no eol/text attribute today; this is
# not a fix for a live problem). NOTE (ship-review finding, PR #7186): attribute resolution is
# last-match-wins, so this protection holds only against rules ABOVE this point in the file, not
# against a future rule appended AFTER it — exactly how this SD's own NUL-byte escapes below
# override the broad *.js pin above them. A future `*.png text eol=lf`-shaped rule appended below
# this block would still coerce these extensions. Keep new broad text/eol rules ABOVE this block,
# or add a matching binary/-text re-pin after them if that is ever unavoidable.
*.png binary
*.webm binary
*.pptx binary
*.docx binary
*.gz binary

# SD-LEO-INFRA-REPO-WIDE-GITATTRIBUTES-001 — these 2 paths contain literal NUL bytes (by
# design — test/production fixtures for NUL-byte handling) and were already picking up
# `text eol=lf` from the broad *.js pin above (line 26), exposing them to a forced
# --renormalize rewrite of binary content on any future renormalization pass. Escaping with
# a bare `binary`/`-text` mark is NOT sufficient — that clears `text` but measurably leaves
# `eol=lf` residual (git ls-files --eol still reports it), which still trips both
# tests/static-guards/eol-crlf-ratchet.test.js and scripts/lint/eol-renormalization-lint.mjs.
# The only escape form measured to fully clear both attributes is `-text eol=` (empty value).
# Ordered last (after the broad *.js pin) so last-match-wins precedence applies.
lib/income/first-revenue-rollup-aggregator.js -text eol=
tests/unit/solomon-advisory-no-literal-nul.test.js -text eol=

# QF-20260902-487 -- renormalize the 197-path mixed-index EOL set (upstream commit 57cfb62b
# HOUSEKEEPING-WEEKLY-REPORT-001 exposed paths whose INDEX still carried CRLF on this clone,
# reading as phantom modifications in the shared root: 729+/729- raw, zero content lines under
# git diff --ignore-all-space). Pinned individually (not a broad *.md rule) because 69 OTHER
# .md files repo-wide are pure-CRLF (not mixed) and out of this fix's scope per
# SD-LEO-INFRA-REPO-WIDE-GITATTRIBUTES-001's exclusion of .md until its crlf/mixed count is
# fully zero -- only the mixed-index set below was renormalized in this PR.
.claude/commands/triangulation-protocol.md text eol=lf
